From 3b4876e03945004ab56097830aa409d604c33c21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anders Jensen-Waud <5827908+ajensenwaud@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:53:28 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] ricursive: Ripple-65B-Preview on CUDA, EXL3 packs on one
 GB10 (sm_121)

The ricursive family's CUDA forward over an ExLlamaV3 pack: main's exl3.Dense for every projection and the head,
norms.rms, qk_rows (per-head q/k norm, NeoX RoPE on sliding layers, none on the NoPE full layers, the cache
writes) and window_attention's head-dim-128 rows (sliding window 1024 keys including the row, full layers whole).
ricursive.cu adds only Ripple's own steps: the bf16 embedding scale, the value residual (V's input is
lerp(scaled embedding, normed hidden, sigmoid(value_res_lambda))), the fp32 sigmoid gate over g_proj, the
clamped SwiGLU (gate <= 20, up within +-20) and PeriNorm's post-attention/post-FFN norms into the fp32 residual.
config.zig (#2231) admits EXL3 packs; the loader cuts a rank's share by columns (q/k/v/g, gate/up, head) or rows
(o, down) on 128-wide blocks for TP=2, which admit() still refuses with every SM but 121.
tf-ricursive (zig build ricursive-cuda) writes prompt-path, decode-path, greedy and per-layer rows for the
exllamav3 parity check.
